.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt PROCESS_VM_READV 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm process_vm_readv ,
.Nm process_vm_writev
.Nd copy between the memory of the caller and of another process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/uio.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn process_vm_readv "pid_t pid" "const struct iovec *local_iov" "unsigned long liovcnt" "const struct iovec *remote_iov" "unsigned long riovcnt" "unsigned long flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn process_vm_writev "pid_t pid" "const struct iovec *local_iov" "unsigned long liovcnt" "const struct iovec *remote_iov" "unsigned long riovcnt" "unsigned long flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn process_vm_readv
copies from the buffers
.Fa remote_iov
names in the memory of the process or thread
.Fa pid
into the caller's buffers
.Fa local_iov ,
and
.Fn process_vm_writev
the other way, as Linux 6.12's calls do.
strace reads the strings of a traced program with the first.
Each side's buffers are taken in order, the bytes running from one buffer
of a side into the next, and the copy ends when either side's buffers end.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Pp
The caller may reach the memory of a process whose address space it
shares, of its own thread group, or, as
.Xr ptrace 2
lets it attach, of any process when it is privileged, and otherwise of a
process whose real, effective and saved user and group IDs are each the
caller's real user and group ID, which is dumpable and not privileged.
Unlike a tracer's access, the copy reads only what the target may read
and writes only what it may write: a page of a mapping without that
access, of a guard region or of device memory ends the copy.
.Pp
Each side has at most 1024 buffers, and the total of the caller's is cut
to 2147479552 bytes, as Linux cuts a read or write.
With no byte in the caller's buffers, or none in the remote ones, the
call returns 0 at once, before it looks for
.Fa pid .
.Ss System call interface
The system call numbers are 347 and 348 on i386, and 310 and 311 on
x86-64.
On the x86-64 kernel a 32-bit program's
.Vt struct iovec
is two 32-bit words.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The bytes copied.
When a page of either side fails after some bytes were copied, the call
returns those, which reach every byte before the failing page; it
returns -1 and sets
.Va errno
only when it copied none.
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/limitsprobe.c
reads a string from its child, writes into the child's memory and reads
it back, and is refused the memory of a process of another user.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOMEM]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A vector cannot be read, a buffer of the caller runs past user space, or
the first page to copy could not be read or written, on either side.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
is not 0, a side has more than 1024 buffers, or a length is negative as a
signed size.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel has no room to copy the vectors.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not reach the memory of
.Fa pid .
.It Bq Er ESRCH
.Fa pid
names no process or thread the caller can see, or one without an address
space.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Xr readv 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
